NAME
    id - print this session's user identity

SYNOPSIS
    id

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the calling task's user and group id, with names resolved from
    /etc/passwd and /etc/group (numeric fallback when a file or id is missing):

        uid=1000(user) gid=1000(user)

    Identity is carried by the kernel per task and inherited across spawn, so
    running id after su reports the new user - the proof that a command runs as
    whoever started it:

        # id
        uid=0(root) gid=0(root)
        # su user
        $ id
        uid=1000(user) gid=1000(user)

    A pipeline stage like any other: `id | grep uid` works.

SEE ALSO
    su, sh
